# TOURNAMENTS

> The scoreboard answers one question.
>
> Who is the best player right now?
>
> The tournament answers another.
>
> Who gets paid for it?

The arena operates through recurring competitive rounds.

Every round creates an opportunity.

Every round creates a champion.

Every champion eventually gets replaced.

---

# 🏆 WHAT IS A TOURNAMENT?

A tournament is a timed competitive session where players attempt to achieve the highest validated score before the round ends.

Simple.

Play.

Compete.

Climb.

Win.

When the clock hits zero, everything changes.

---

# ⏱ ROUND DURATION

Tournament duration is configurable by the arena.

Typical tournament durations range between:

### 15–30 Minutes

The arena may adjust tournament duration based on:

- Community activity
- Participation levels
- Ecosystem conditions
- Competitive engagement

The active duration of a round should always be visible to participants.

Nobody should have to guess how much time is left.

---

# 🔄 TOURNAMENT LIFE CYCLE

Every round follows the same process.

```text
ROUND OPENS
      ↓
PLAYERS COMPETE
      ↓
SCORES SUBMITTED
      ↓
SCORES VALIDATED
      ↓
LEADERBOARD UPDATES
      ↓
TIMER EXPIRES
      ↓
LEADERBOARD LOCKS
      ↓
WINNER CONFIRMED
      ↓
REWARD PROCESSED
      ↓
NEW ROUND OPENS
```

Simple.

Predictable.

Auditable.

---

# 🚀 ROUND START

When a round begins:

- The tournament becomes active
- The leaderboard accepts scores
- New submissions become eligible
- Players compete for position

The arena is open.

Good luck.

---

# 📊 DURING THE ROUND

Players may submit multiple runs during an active tournament.

Only valid competitive scores are eligible.

If a player improves their score:

The leaderboard updates.

If a player takes first place:

Everyone else suddenly has a problem.

---

# 🏁 LEADERBOARD LOCK

When the timer reaches zero:

## The leaderboard locks.

No new scores become eligible for that tournament.

No extensions.

No last-second exceptions.

No:

> "Bro my internet lagged."

The lock time applies equally to everyone.

The round ends when the round ends.

---

# ✅ SCORE VALIDATION

Tournament results depend on validated scores.

Not merely submitted scores.

The competitive system may validate:

- Session integrity
- Gameplay legitimacy
- Submission authenticity
- Eligibility requirements

This helps ensure that rewards are determined by legitimate competitive performance.

Not creativity in developer tools.

---

# 👑 WINNER DETERMINATION

The champion of a tournament is:

## The player holding the highest validated score when the tournament closes.

Higher score wins.

That's the rule.

No voting.

No committee.

No popularity contest.

The scoreboard decides.

---

# ⚖️ TIE BREAKING

Sometimes two players will achieve the exact same score.

When that happens:

## First validated score wins.

Example:

```text
Player A : 420
Validated at 01:04 PM

Player B : 420
Validated at 01:07 PM
```

Player A wins.

Identical scores are ranked by validation timestamp.

The earlier validated score takes priority.

This keeps competition simple and avoids reward disputes.

---

# 💰 REWARD ELIGIBILITY

Winning a tournament may make a player eligible for the corresponding tournament reward.

Reward processing follows the reward rules defined by the ecosystem.

Tournament results determine who won.

Reward infrastructure determines how rewards are handled.

Those systems work together.

But they remain separate.

---

# 🏅 CHAMPIONS

Every completed tournament creates a champion.

Champions become part of arena history.

A champion is remembered for:

- Tournament victory
- Winning score
- Tournament timestamp
- Historical rankings
- Championship count

Winning once is impressive.

Winning repeatedly becomes a problem for everyone else.

---

# 📖 ARENA HISTORY

The arena keeps score.

Not just during tournaments.

Across tournaments.

Historical records may include:

- Previous champions
- Highest scores
- Winning streaks
- Record holders
- Historic leaderboard battles
- Tournament archives

Every tournament leaves a footprint.

Wraith remembers.

---

# 🤖 WRAITH'S ROLE

Wraith does not determine winners.

Wraith does not validate scores.

Wraith does not modify rankings.

The machine handles that.

Wraith reacts to the results.

Examples:

### NEW CHAMPION

> "The throne has changed hands."

---

### CLOSE FINISH

> "That was uncomfortably close."

---

### DOMINANT VICTORY

> "Someone check on second place."

---

### LONG WINNING STREAK

> "This is becoming a community problem."

---

The results create the story.

Wraith tells it.

---

# 🛡 FAIRNESS

The competitive system is built around one principle:

## Everybody competes under the same rules.

Tournament integrity requires:

- Transparent timing
- Consistent scoring
- Deterministic ranking
- Score validation
- Clear tie handling
- Publicly understandable rules

The objective is simple:

If someone wins a tournament...

Everyone should understand why.

---

# ⚠️ TOURNAMENT PARTICIPATION

Participation in tournaments does not guarantee:

- Victory
- Rewards
- Ranking
- Future eligibility

Competition exists because not everybody can win.

Fortunately.

Otherwise the leaderboard would be extremely confusing.

---

# 🔟 TOURNAMENT RULES

### RULE 01

A tournament begins when the arena opens it.

### RULE 02

A tournament ends when the timer expires.

### RULE 03

Only validated scores are eligible.

### RULE 04

Higher score ranks higher.

### RULE 05

The leaderboard determines ranking.

### RULE 06

The leaderboard locks at tournament close.

### RULE 07

The highest validated score wins.

### RULE 08

Ties are broken by validation timestamp.

### RULE 09

First validated score wins the tie.

### RULE 10

Wraith comments on the results.

Wraith does not decide them.
